figures.caption_en:
Figure 3: Balmer-break color versus \(n=2\) neutral hydrogen column density for all 11 LRDs. The Balmer-break color \(f_{
u,5500}/f_{
u,3600}\) measures the Balmer-break strength, with larger values indicating a stronger break Matthee et al. (2026) . The left and right panels show it as a function of \(N_{HI,\mathit{n}=2}\) from the homogeneous and clumpy fits. Each object has a unique marker shape and the same color as in Fig. 2 . Opaque markers show \(N_{HI,\mathit{n}=2}\) from the H \( eta\) fit with the prior from the H \(\alpha\) fit, and faded markers show the H \(\alpha\) fit without the prior. The solid (dashed) lines represent the correlation between \(\log(f_{
u,5500}/f_{
u,3600})\) versus \(\log\,N_{HI,\mathit{n}=2}\) from H \( eta\) (H \(\alpha\) ) fit. Pearson ( \(r\) , in log–log space) and Spearman ( \(ho\) ) coefficients are given in each panel. For H \( eta\) , the homogeneous model shows no correlation ( \(r=+0.01\) , \(ho=-0.05\) ), while the clumpy model shows a positive one ( \(r=+0.64\) , \(ho=+0.62\) , \(p\approx 0.04\) ). For H \(\alpha\) alone, neither model shows a significant correlation (clumpy \(r=+0.25\) , \(ho=+0.04\) ; homogeneous \(r=-0.06\) , \(ho=-0.11\) ); the clumpy slope is positive but not significant.

